# Configuration for the bridge and the demo client.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Everything here is a LIGHTNINGFEED_* variable, which is the same prefix the
# command-line flags read from and which overrides them. That is the whole
# configuration mechanism: there is no config file to parse, and nothing to keep
# in sync with the flags.
#
# The three region variables are the ones most people change. All three, or none:
# supplying only some is an error rather than a silent world-wide feed, so a typo
# cannot quietly turn a regional deployment into a global one.

# --- region ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# All three of these, or none of them. Left empty, the bridge publishes every
# stroke on Earth; set, it publishes only those within the radius.
#
# Empty is the default here on purpose. A world-wide feed shows something the
# moment it starts, and `make demo` therefore works on a fresh clone. The region
# below is a quiet 10 km circle in Crete that can go hours without a stroke, so a
# demo pointed at it would usually show nothing at all — which looks exactly like a
# broken install rather than like a quiet sky. Put your own coordinates in to
# narrow the feed.
#
# A radius is capped at 2500 km and the cell enumeration at 2 million cells; the
# cell count is the one that bites, since a degree of longitude covers fewer
# kilometres the further you get from the equator.

# A label for logs and metrics. Optional: without it the coordinates are used.
#LIGHTNINGFEED_REGION_NAME=roussospiti
#LIGHTNINGFEED_REGION_LAT=35.3340688
#LIGHTNINGFEED_REGION_LON=24.4944483
#LIGHTNINGFEED_REGION_RADIUS_KM=10

# Whether a stroke whose position is genuinely uncertain counts as inside.
#
#   include  (default)  dist - dev <= radius <= dist + dev counts as inside
#   exclude              only strokes certainly inside the radius count
#
# A missed stroke is worse than a slightly misplaced one, which is why include is
# the default; exclude is one variable away and needs no rebuild, because the band
# is carried on every message and stored on every row.
LIGHTNINGFEED_BOUNDARY_POLICY=include

# --- broker ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Inside the compose network the broker is reachable as "nats".
LIGHTNINGFEED_NATS_URL=nats://nats:4222
LIGHTNINGFEED_STREAM=LIGHTNING

# --- upstream -------------------------------------------------------------

# The live feed. lightningmaps.org and its two servers, live and live2, issue
# independent id sequences, so switching this deliberately is a cold start: the
# bridge warns and drops the stored cursor rather than resuming across the
# boundary. There is no failover URL, on purpose.
LIGHTNINGFEED_UPSTREAM_URL=wss://live.lightningmaps.org:443/

# Which networks to subscribe to, as a bitmask:
#   1 reserved (carries no data)   2 Blitzortung.org   4 LightningMaps.org   8 test
# 4 is the upstream's own default and what this project ships with.
LIGHTNINGFEED_SRC_MASK=4

# --- archive --------------------------------------------------------------

# The SQLite file. It belongs to the leader, which the leadership lease
# guarantees is exactly one process.
LIGHTNINGFEED_SQLITE=/var/lib/lightningfeed/lightningfeed.db

# How long strokes stay in SQLite. The JetStream stream keeps two hours; longer
# history lives here, and radius queries read this and not NATS, because a radius
# query is not something subject filtering can express.
LIGHTNINGFEED_RETENTION=168h

# --- rarely tuned ---------------------------------------------------------

# Three attempts with a pause between them, then the process gives up. The
# absorbed thing is a broker hiccup measured in milliseconds; a sustained outage
# still stops the pipeline, because continuing would mean discarding every stroke
# silently. Uncomment only to change the defaults.
#LIGHTNINGFEED_PUBLISH_ATTEMPTS=3
#LIGHTNINGFEED_PUBLISH_BACKOFF=250ms

# Whether to take a leadership lease before connecting upstream. Turning this off
# is right for exactly one process on a machine of its own and wrong for two,
# which would then both open a connection the upstream will not let them hold.
#LIGHTNINGFEED_LEADER_ELECT=true

# Everything else has a flag but no variable: --reconnect-min, --reconnect-max,
# --idle-timeout, --handshake-timeout, --backfill-drop, --dedupe-ttl,
# --prune-every, --leader-key. Their defaults are measured rather than guessed,
# and the reasons are in the README's "Operational constraints".

# --- observability --------------------------------------------------------

# Prometheus metrics and readiness. Also where the container healthcheck looks,
# so changing this means changing the Dockerfile's HEALTHCHECK too.
LIGHTNINGFEED_METRICS_ADDR=:9109
LIGHTNINGFEED_LOG_LEVEL=info
LIGHTNINGFEED_LOG_FORMAT=json